1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
Imagine a bus pulling away from a

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,599
stop. Its speed rises from zero to

3
00:00:04,599 --> 00:00:07,069
two metres per second in one second,

4
00:00:07,069 --> 00:00:09,113
then to four. If the same speed

5
00:00:09,113 --> 00:00:12,690
increase happens each second, the acceleration is

6
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:15,926
constant. Acceleration is not speed itself; it

7
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:18,992
tells us how quickly velocity changes over

8
00:00:18,992 --> 00:00:21,377
time. In this lesson we will choose

9
00:00:21,377 --> 00:00:24,273
the right formula, solve one example, read

10
00:00:24,273 --> 00:00:27,168
a velocity-time graph, and work through a

11
00:00:27,168 --> 00:00:31,086
problem from the supplied Testcenter-3 collection. At

12
00:00:31,086 --> 00:00:33,471
the end, try a short check question

13
00:00:33,471 --> 00:00:34,152
yourself.

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,704
Start a solution by listing the known

15
00:00:45,704 --> 00:00:48,495
quantities. Let v naught be the initial

16
00:00:48,495 --> 00:00:51,025
velocity, v the final velocity, t the

17
00:00:51,025 --> 00:00:53,380
time, a the acceleration, and s the

18
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,956
displacement. Velocity is measured in metres per

19
00:00:56,956 --> 00:01:00,619
second, acceleration in metres per second squared,

20
00:01:00,619 --> 00:01:03,759
time in seconds, and displacement in metres.

21
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:07,074
Displacement is the directed change from the

22
00:01:07,074 --> 00:01:10,824
starting position to the ending position. Distance

23
00:01:10,824 --> 00:01:12,918
is the full length of the path.

24
00:01:12,918 --> 00:01:15,796
If the object never turns back, distance

25
00:01:15,796 --> 00:01:18,936
equals the magnitude of displacement. If it

26
00:01:18,936 --> 00:01:22,425
reverses, split the motion into sections. Choose

27
00:01:22,425 --> 00:01:25,216
the positive axis before calculating.

28
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,271
For straight-line motion with constant acceleration, final

29
00:01:30,271 --> 00:01:32,866
velocity equals v naught plus a times

30
00:01:32,866 --> 00:01:35,127
t. With our chosen axis, positive a

31
00:01:35,127 --> 00:01:38,560
increases the velocity component; negative a can

32
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,742
reduce it. Displacement equals v naught times

33
00:01:41,742 --> 00:01:43,501
t plus one half a t squared.

34
00:01:43,501 --> 00:01:47,101
Use these equations only while acceleration stays

35
00:01:47,101 --> 00:01:49,948
constant. If the object turns around, keep

36
00:01:49,948 --> 00:01:52,628
the sign of displacement and split the

37
00:01:52,628 --> 00:01:55,224
path to find total distance. Check the

38
00:01:55,224 --> 00:01:58,741
units after substitution: metres per second times

39
00:01:58,741 --> 00:02:02,006
seconds gives metres, and acceleration must be

40
00:02:02,006 --> 00:02:04,016
in metres per second squared.

41
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,403
Let us solve an example. A cyclist

42
00:02:11,403 --> 00:02:14,785
starts at two metres per second. Acceleration

43
00:02:14,785 --> 00:02:17,365
is one point five metres per second

44
00:02:17,365 --> 00:02:20,213
squared, and time is four seconds. Final

45
00:02:20,213 --> 00:02:22,794
velocity is two plus one point five

46
00:02:22,794 --> 00:02:25,820
times four, which equals eight metres per

47
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:29,023
second. Displacement is two times four plus

48
00:02:29,023 --> 00:02:32,138
one point five times four squared, divided

49
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:34,274
by two. The first part is eight

50
00:02:34,274 --> 00:02:37,834
metres; the second is twelve. Total displacement

51
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:40,771
is twenty metres. Check it using average

52
00:02:40,771 --> 00:02:43,262
velocity: half of two plus eight is

53
00:02:43,262 --> 00:02:46,110
five metres per second. Five times four

54
00:02:46,110 --> 00:02:47,712
is also twenty metres.

55
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,916
On a velocity-time graph, the slope gives

56
00:02:54,916 --> 00:02:58,690
acceleration: change in velocity divided by elapsed

57
00:02:58,690 --> 00:03:01,434
time. The signed area beneath the graph

58
00:03:01,434 --> 00:03:05,036
gives displacement. In our example, velocity rises

59
00:03:05,036 --> 00:03:07,524
from two to eight metres per second

60
00:03:07,524 --> 00:03:10,611
over four seconds. Split the trapezoid into

61
00:03:10,611 --> 00:03:13,527
a rectangle and a triangle. The rectangle

62
00:03:13,527 --> 00:03:15,843
is two times four, or eight metres.

63
00:03:15,843 --> 00:03:18,073
The triangle is one half times a

64
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:19,960
base of four and a height of

65
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:22,790
six, or twelve metres. Together they give

66
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:25,534
twenty metres. If the graph drops below

67
00:03:25,534 --> 00:03:28,622
the time axis, displacement can be negative;

68
00:03:28,622 --> 00:03:31,109
add the magnitudes of areas to find

69
00:03:31,109 --> 00:03:32,224
total distance.

70
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,919
Now read question one from the supplied

71
00:03:37,919 --> 00:03:40,838
test collection. From zero to one second,

72
00:03:40,838 --> 00:03:43,492
velocity rises from zero to four; the

73
00:03:43,492 --> 00:03:46,146
triangle area is two metres. From one

74
00:03:46,146 --> 00:03:49,065
to three seconds, velocity stays at four,

75
00:03:49,065 --> 00:03:51,807
so the rectangle area is eight metres.

76
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:54,284
During the last second it falls to

77
00:03:54,284 --> 00:03:57,291
zero, adding another two metres. The total

78
00:03:57,291 --> 00:03:59,326
is two plus eight plus two, or

79
00:03:59,326 --> 00:04:02,510
twelve metres. The printed key marks answer

80
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:05,075
B. Do not mistake the final velocity

81
00:04:05,075 --> 00:04:07,995
for the distance travelled: a zero speed

82
00:04:07,995 --> 00:04:10,029
at the end does not erase the

83
00:04:10,029 --> 00:04:12,152
earlier area under the graph.

84
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,995
Your turn. Speed falls from eighteen to

85
00:04:20,995 --> 00:04:23,897
six metres per second in four seconds.

86
00:04:23,897 --> 00:04:27,548
Acceleration is six minus eighteen, divided by

87
00:04:27,548 --> 00:04:30,918
four: minus three metres per second squared.

88
00:04:30,918 --> 00:04:33,913
The object does not reverse, so average

89
00:04:33,913 --> 00:04:38,032
velocity is twelve and displacement is forty-eight

90
00:04:38,032 --> 00:04:41,214
metres. Remember the two core equations: v

91
00:04:41,214 --> 00:04:43,086
equals v naught plus a t; s

92
00:04:43,086 --> 00:04:45,426
equals v naught t plus one half

93
00:04:45,426 --> 00:04:47,860
a t squared. Kinematics is in the

94
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:51,792
2026 UNT physics specification, which gives about

95
00:04:51,792 --> 00:04:54,693
two minutes per task on average. Label

96
00:04:54,693 --> 00:04:57,876
the quantities first, then check the sign,

97
00:04:57,876 --> 00:04:59,280
formula, and units.
